{sec("white", f"""{split("", f"Direct Means Less{G}", "Direct relationships are not about removing every intermediary. They are about reducing unnecessary layers, keeping responsibilities clear and giving clients a more transparent view of the operating chain.")}<div class="big3 mt"><div class="it rv"><b>Less Cost.</b><p>A more direct operating structure can reduce avoidable intermediary layers and duplicated charges. Actual pricing depends on the service, asset, account and third-party costs.</p></div><div class="it rv"><b>Less Complexity.</b><p>Fewer handovers can make instructions easier to coordinate and exceptions easier to identify. The relevant service providers and market rules still apply.</p></div><div class="it rv"><b>Less Exposure.</b><p>For eligible clients, individually segregated depository accounts can keep securities separately identifiable rather than pooled with unrelated client holdings in an omnibus account.</p></div></div><p class="fine" style="margin-top:22px">Segregation can reduce certain cross-client operational and asset-identification risks. It does not eliminate market, counterparty, depository, banking or insolvency risk, and does not guarantee recovery in a default.</p><a class="tlink" href="settlement-custody.html">How EMFI&rsquo;s settlement and custody arrangements work</a>""")}
